      <p>UNESCO reports that more than 7,000 languages are spoken globally and that at least 40% of the estimated 7,000 languages are endangered. UNESCO also emphasizes that languages transmit knowledge, ideas, beliefs, traditions, and cultural identity between generations.</p>

      <p>This is one reason learning even a small phrase from another language can be meaningful. You are not simply replacing one English sentence with another set of sounds; you are learning how another community expresses a particular idea.</p>

      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;opacity:0.8;"><strong>Source:</strong> UNESCO, International Decade of Indigenous Languages.</p>

      <h2>1. Why Cultural Awareness Matters in Global Communication</h2>
      <p>Every language is shaped by history, values, and traditions. When you use culturally appropriate phrases, it shows you respect the other person’s background — and they’ll likely open up more easily.</p>

      <h3>Respect Creates Connection</h3>
      <p>Imagine meeting someone who greets you using a phrase from your language — it instantly feels special, right? That’s how powerful respect can be. Learning small cultural phrases is not about showing off, but about showing care. The service enables users to see each other while talking through <a href="https://wakiee.live">Random Video Call</a>.</p>

      <h3>Avoiding Misunderstandings</h3>
      <p>Direct translations sometimes miss the emotional intent behind a phrase — in English, "How are you?" is usually just a greeting, not a real question about your health, but in some cultures it's taken literally and answered with an actual update. Knowing which default you're talking to can save you from an oddly serious answer to a casual question.</p>

      <h3>Building Global Friendships</h3>
      <p>When you talk to people on the platform, you might meet someone from Japan one moment and from France the next. Cultural phrases act as universal keys — helping you connect instantly, even if you don’t share the same native tongue.</p>

      <h2>Not Every Phrase Has a One-to-One Translation</h2>

      <p>A useful cultural phrase should be learned together with its context. Knowing the literal translation is not always enough because the appropriate level of formality, relationship between speakers, and situation can change how an expression is used.</p>

